CHAPTER II
REVIEW OF RELATED LITERATURE AND STUDIES
Local Literature
Training Regulations and Curriculum Guide for Computer Systems Servicing NC II
The Technical Education and Skills Development Authority (TESDA) and the Department of Education (DepEd) (2024) established the national curriculum standards for Computer Systems Servicing NC II. The regulations outline core technical competencies including computer assembly, network setup, server configuration, and system maintenance. The curriculum emphasizes standardized procedures and checklist-based assessments to prepare senior high school learners for national certification.
This regulatory standard provides the competency baseline for this study on Grade 11 CSS students at NCST. While the curriculum mandates server and network servicing, institutional evaluations primarily focus on basic assembly and linear checklists. This research examines why student proficiency drops during non-routine system faults and applies SYSMOD to strengthen the diagnostic competencies mandated by TESDA.
Policy-Standard to Enhance Quality Assurance in Philippine Higher Education Through Outcomes-Based QA
The Commission on Higher Education (CHED) (2012) issued Memorandum Order No. 46, mandating Outcomes-Based Education (OBE) across Philippine technical programs. The policy directs institutions to transition from measuring educational inputs to assessing demonstrable student competencies, authentic performance tasks, and practical problem-solving capabilities required in industry.
This policy directly supports the pedagogical framework of the present study. In alignment with outcomes-based principles, this investigation evaluates actual student diagnostic performance during simulated technical breakdowns rather than relying on paper-based assessments. The proposed SYSMOD framework provides a structured tool to achieve measurable diagnostic outcomes in computer servicing.




Miseducation: The Current State of Philippine Education (Year One Report)
The Second Congressional Commission on Education (EDCOM II) (2024) evaluated the systemic challenges facing Philippine technical-vocational education. The report documented widespread shortages of functional laboratory equipment, outdated training facilities, and gaps in student practical skills across secondary TVL tracks.
These findings directly validate the institutional background of this research at NCST. Due to equipment constraints and the risk of damaging hardware, school laboratories rarely expose learners to real-world system failures. This study addresses this problem by integrating virtualized sandboxes and systematic state modeling, allowing students to troubleshoot complex faults without risking physical computers.
Policy Guidelines on Classroom Assessment for the K to 12 Basic Education Program
The Department of Education (DepEd) (2015) issued DepEd Order No. 8, establishing assessment guidelines that emphasize formative assessment, performance tasks, and authentic skills evaluation. The policy highlights the need to monitor how students apply conceptual knowledge to resolve real-world tasks and practical problems.
This assessment guideline informs the mixed-methods evaluation employed in this study. The research utilizes rubric-based diagnostic performance tracking to observe how Grade 11 CSS learners isolate faults during simulated hardware and software incidents, supporting DepEd's goal of authentic competency assessment.
Electronic Media and Computer-Aided Instructional Models in Technical Education
Foundational local literature by Dela Cerna (2000) and Gonzales (2002) examined the integration of computer-aided instructional media in technical and vocational courses. Their works demonstrated that visual diagrams, interactive simulations, and structured technical guides enhance student retention and psychomotor skill acquisition compared to lecture-only instruction.
These foundational studies support the visual and structural approach of this capstone research. By adapting SYSMOD diagramming and visual state machines for computer repair, this study equips Grade 11 learners with an accessible conceptual map to trace system interactions and diagnose faults systematically.
Local Studies
Teaching Computer Systems Servicing Competencies on Students' Skills Development and Practical Learning Application
In an empirical study conducted in Laguna, Padilla (2026) evaluated the technical performance of secondary TVL-ICT students across core CSS competencies. The findings revealed that students scored high in routine hardware assembly and basic cable crimping, but experienced substantial difficulty when performing independent fault diagnosis and non-routine system repairs.
Padilla's study provides direct regional evidence of the diagnostic deficit investigated at NCST. Grade 11 CSS learners exhibit strong assembly skills but lack systematic problem-solving methods when unexpected errors occur. This directly justifies introducing the SYSMOD framework to guide students through step-by-step hypothesis testing during complex repairs.
Pedagogical Flexibility and Adaptive Teaching Strategies for Practical Skills Mastery in Computer Systems Servicing
Bascuguin and Generoso (2026) conducted a mixed-methods study in Batangas examining public school CSS instruction. Their study revealed that while teachers adopt adaptive instructional strategies, hands-on learning is heavily restricted by static laboratory setups and a lack of authentic breakdown scenarios because school computers must remain operational for daily classes.
This study substantiates the existence of the Clean Lab paradox within Region IV-A classrooms. To protect laboratory equipment, schools prevent students from experiencing dynamic system failures, leaving them unprepared for real-world troubleshooting. The present study resolves this issue by combining SYSMOD modeling with virtualized sandboxes to simulate live faults safely.
Development and Acceptability of CSS Droid Mobile Learning Material in Computer Hardware Servicing
Virtudazo (2022) developed and validated an interactive mobile learning tool called CSS Droid for computer servicing students. The application incorporated component visualization, self-paced guides, and simulated repair modules, resulting in significant improvements in student technical knowledge, engagement, and practical task performance.
Virtudazo's findings demonstrate the effectiveness of structured digital learning aids in technical education. While CSS Droid focused primarily on hardware identification and basic assembly, this study expands upon that concept by applying structured system modeling to advanced software diagnostics, event log analysis, and network fault isolation.
Supplementary YouTube Videos as Instructional Materials to Enhance Computer Troubleshooting and Repair Techniques
Caratiquit and Caratiquit (2022) evaluated the use of curated video demonstrations as supplementary learning resources in TVL computer troubleshooting classes. The experimental results indicated that students provided with structured visual walkthroughs achieved significantly higher diagnostic scores and required less instructor intervention than those using text-only modules.
This research confirms that vocational learners benefit greatly from structured visual guidance when learning complex repair procedures. The current study builds on this insight by providing students with visual SYSMOD state transition diagrams and clear diagnostic algorithms to guide their troubleshooting decisions.
CSS Droid: An Android-Based Computer System Servicing Training App with Virtual Computer Hardware Assembly
Paytaren (2020) developed a virtual computer assembly application at Batangas State University designed to address laboratory equipment shortages. The application enabled students to practice virtual component assembly and basic diagnostic checks, receiving high validation ratings from technical experts and vocational teachers.
Paytaren's project highlights the practical value of simulated training environments in overcoming institutional equipment limitations. The present study extends this approach from basic physical assembly to advanced logical troubleshooting, utilizing virtualized environments where students diagnose operating system crashes and network outages safely.
Foreign Literature
Incorporating Model-Based Systems Engineering into Computer Engineering Laboratory Instruction
Rashid (2020) analyzed the integration of Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) into technical laboratory curricula. Rashid argued that conventional trial-and-error troubleshooting in technical education is inefficient and leads to cognitive frustration, proposing that modeling systems as interconnected functional blocks promotes structured, hypothesis-driven problem-solving.
Rashid's work provides the foundational academic rationale for adopting systems modeling in this study. Rather than allowing Grade 11 CSS students to guess randomly when troubleshooting, the proposed framework uses SYSMOD principles to help students visualize system dependencies and isolate faulty subsystems systematically.
Interventions for Technical Debugging and Fault Isolation Instruction: A Systematic Literature Review
Yang, Zhang, and Wang (2024) conducted a comprehensive literature review of technical debugging interventions across international technical institutions. The authors found that unstructured trial-and-error leads to cognitive overload and persistent diagnostic errors among novice learners, whereas explicit instructional frameworks and hypothesis-testing models significantly enhance diagnostic speed and accuracy.
This systematic review directly supports the core objective of the present study. Unstructured troubleshooting among Grade 11 CSS learners is an established cognitive limitation of novice technicians. Implementing a structured framework like SYSMOD directly addresses this barrier by replacing guesswork with systematic root-cause analysis.
SYSMOD – The Systems Modeling Toolbox: Pragmatic MBSE with SysML
Weilkiens (2016) developed the Systems Modeling Toolbox (SYSMOD), establishing pragmatic methods for modeling complex systems using SysML. SYSMOD defines system boundaries, subsystem interactions, and state transitions, allowing engineers to trace signals and identify where expected behaviors diverge into fault states.
Weilkiens' methodology serves as the primary theoretical framework for this research. In this study, SYSMOD concepts are adapted for secondary CSS education, framing a personal computer as interconnected hardware and software subsystems so that Grade 11 students can track faults logically rather than through blind component swapping.
Outcomes-Based Education and Constructive Alignment in Technical Instruction
Spady (1994) and Biggs and Tang (2013) established the principles of Outcomes-Based Education and constructive alignment in technical training. They posited that learning outcomes, classroom instructional activities, and practical performance assessments must be harmonized to ensure students develop transferable analytical competencies.
This pedagogical literature provides the instructional design basis for this study. The proposed SYSMOD framework aligns classroom troubleshooting lessons with authentic diagnostic assessments, ensuring that Grade 11 CSS students demonstrate systematic problem-solving skills aligned with vocational industry expectations.
Cognitive Load Theory and the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research
Sweller (2011) and Damschroder et al. (2022) formulated Cognitive Load Theory and implementation research models. Sweller demonstrated that unguided problem-solving overloads working memory in novice learners, while Damschroder emphasized the importance of contextual adaptability when introducing structured interventions into educational settings.
These theoretical models explain why novice CSS students default to panic, random swapping, or immediate hard drive reformatting during complex failures. The SYSMOD framework acts as a cognitive scaffold, breaking technical problems into manageable steps and reducing mental overload during diagnostics.
Foreign Studies
Psychomotor and Troubleshooting Skill Development in Network Operating System Courses Using Raspberry Pi
Puspanda and Budiyanto (2021) investigated the development of troubleshooting skills among technical students using modular Raspberry Pi hardware and network operating systems. Their experimental findings demonstrated that hands-on, modular troubleshooting exercises significantly improved student fault-isolation speed and practical psychomotor performance compared to traditional lecture instruction.
This foreign study supports the methodological design of the present research. It confirms that active engagement with hands-on technical environments builds diagnostic confidence, reinforcing the value of integrating structured diagnostic exercises into Grade 11 CSS laboratory training at NCST.
Development and Assessment of a Model-Based Embedded Systems Laboratory Course
Sababha, AlQaralleh, and Alqudah (2021) evaluated the implementation of model-based instruction in technical laboratory courses. The quantitative results revealed that students trained with system modeling frameworks achieved higher troubleshooting accuracy, identified root causes faster, and demonstrated superior retention of diagnostic procedures compared to cohorts taught via traditional trial-and-error.
The empirical findings of Sababha et al. provide direct evidence that model-based training improves technical troubleshooting. This strongly reinforces the hypothesis of the current study that introducing SYSMOD modeling to Grade 11 CSS learners will reduce Mean Time to Repair (MTTR) and improve diagnostic competence.
Supporting Secondary School Students' System Modeling Practices Through Scaffolded Technology-Enhanced Curriculum Design
Bielik, Damelin, and Krajcik (2022) examined how secondary school learners utilize system modeling tools to understand complex physical and technical interactions. Their research proved that secondary students can master systems thinking when provided with visual scaffolding, allowing them to trace cause-and-effect relationships and predict system behaviors accurately.
Bielik et al.'s findings validate the developmental appropriateness of introducing systems modeling to senior high school students. It demonstrates that Grade 11 learners possess the cognitive maturity to utilize SYSMOD diagrams to diagnose complex computer and network interactions when provided with structured instructional scaffolding.
Electronics Lab Instructors' Approaches to Troubleshooting Instruction
Dounas-Frazer and Lewandowski (2017) conducted a qualitative study examining how laboratory instructors teach troubleshooting. Their analysis revealed that while instructors recognize troubleshooting as a vital technical skill, instruction is rarely formalized, leaving students to rely on unguided trial-and-error and informal guessing.
This study underscores the instructional gap addressed by the current research. Without explicit, structured diagnostic training, students inevitably develop ad-hoc guessing habits. This research provides an empirical solution by establishing an explicit, teachable troubleshooting framework for secondary CSS classrooms.
Investigating the Impact of Simulation Software on Students' Engagement and Problem-Solving in Technical Vocational Education
Kaysi (2025) evaluated the impact of virtual simulation software on technical-vocational students' diagnostic problem-solving abilities. The study found that virtual simulation environments significantly increased student confidence, eliminated the fear of damaging physical equipment, and allowed learners to practice diagnosing non-routine faults repeatedly.
Kaysi's research confirms the instructional efficacy of virtualized troubleshooting sandboxes. This directly supports the operational design of the current study at NCST, which pairs SYSMOD modeling with virtual tools like VirtualBox and Packet Tracer to provide safe, realistic breakdown scenarios for Grade 11 learners.
Synthesis of the Study
The reviewed local and foreign literature and studies highlight several consistent realities in technical-vocational computer education. First, national curriculum standards from TESDA and DepEd (2024) and regional studies (Padilla, 2026; Bascuguin & Generoso, 2026) establish that students achieve strong competency in routine computer assembly and basic network setup, but experience significant difficulties during complex, non-routine fault diagnosis. Second, educational reports by EDCOM II (2024) and local empirical research confirm that school laboratories operate under the Clean Lab paradox, where computers must be kept strictly operational for daily classes, shielding learners from experiencing live, dynamic breakdowns.
Third, cognitive research (Sweller, 2011; Yang et al., 2024) and observational studies (Dounas-Frazer & Lewandowski, 2017) demonstrate that when students lack structured diagnostic procedures, they suffer from cognitive overload and default to blind trial-and-error, random component swapping, or destructive hard drive reformatting. Finally, international engineering literature (Weilkiens, 2016; Rashid, 2020; Sababha et al., 2021) and simulation studies (Kaysi, 2025; Paytaren, 2020) prove that Model-Based Systems Engineering and virtualized breakdown sandboxes significantly reduce repair times, prevent hardware wear, and elevate troubleshooting from informal guessing into systematic, hypothesis-driven testing.
In synthesis, while local technological interventions have developed mobile guides and instructional videos for basic assembly, no previous Philippine study has applied Model-Based Systems Engineering (SYSMOD) to address advanced troubleshooting deficits in Senior High School Computer Systems Servicing. This research bridges this distinct gap by providing an accessible, standardized diagnostic framework tailored for Grade 11 CSS learners at NCST.


Justification and Gap Bridged
First, this study addresses an unresolved instructional gap in Philippine secondary vocational education. Although regional studies confirm that TVL-ICT students struggle with advanced computer diagnostics, existing research has primarily documented these challenges without providing concrete, standardized frameworks to solve them. This research directly introduces and evaluates an adapted SYSMOD framework to transform student troubleshooting from unguided guessing into structured diagnosis.
Second, the study provides a practical solution to the Clean Lab paradox at the National College of Science and Technology (NCST). By coupling SYSMOD subsystem modeling with zero-cost virtualized simulation sandboxes such as Oracle VirtualBox and Cisco Packet Tracer, NCST educators can provide students with realistic breakdown scenarios without risking institutional computer hardware or incurring spare parts expenses.
Third, the study bridges professional systems engineering concepts with secondary technical training. Traditional vocational instruction often relies on rote memorization and checklists. Adapting SYSMOD principles provides Grade 11 CSS learners with an intuitive cognitive tool to trace system interactions, reduce Mean Time to Repair (MTTR), and develop resilient diagnostic skills essential for national certification and technical industry employment.


